<%
const soc = it.soc.toLowerCase();
const board = it.board.toLowerCase();

function ledValue() {
  switch(soc) {
    case "max78002":
      return "GPIO (P2.4 - LED)";
    case "max78000":
      return "LED1";
    case "max32690":
      return "LED_RED";
    case "max32672":
      if (board === "fthr") {
        return "Red LED";
      } else {
        return "GPIO (P0.22 - LED0)";
      }
    case "max32666":
      if (board === "fthr") {
        return "Red LED";
      } else {
        return "GPIO (P1.14 - LED0)";
      }
    case "max32662":
      return "GPIO (P0.14 - LED0)";
    case "max32660":
      return "GPIO (P0.13 - Red LED)";
    case "max32655":
      return "LED_RED";
    case "max32650":
      if (board === "fthr") {
        return "Red LED";
      } else {
        return "GPIO (P2.25 - LED0)";
      }
    default:
      return "LED0";
  }
}
%>
<%
function timeOut() {
  switch(soc) {
    case "max78002":
      return "600";
    default:
      return "500";
  }
}
%>
<% 
function blinkRate() { 
  return (1000 / (2 * Number(timeOut()))) 
    .toFixed(2) 
    .replace(/\.00$/, '');
} 

%> 
## Description

A basic getting started program.

This version of Hello_World prints an incrementing count to the console UART and toggles <%= ledValue() %> every <%= timeOut() %> ms.

## Software

### Project Usage

Universal instructions on building, flashing, and debugging this project can be found in the **[MSDK User Guide](https://analogdevicesinc.github.io/msdk/USERGUIDE/)**.

### Project-Specific Build Notes

(None - this project builds as a standard example)

## Required Connections

<% if (soc === "max32650") { %>
If using the MAX32650EVKIT (EvKit\_V1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN2 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.

If using the MAX32650FTHR (FTHR\_APPS\_A):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32655") { %>
If using the MAX32655EVKIT (EvKit\_V1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Connect pins JP4(RX_SEL) and JP5(TX_SEL) to RX0 and TX0  header.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP2 (LED0 EN).
-   Close jumper JP3 (LED1 EN).

If using the MAX32655FTHR (FTHR\_Apps\_P1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J4 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32660") { %>
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32662") { %>
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Install headers JP7(RX\_EN) and JP8(TX\_EN).
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32666") { %>
If using the MAX32666EVKIT (EvKit\_V1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN2 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Connect pins JP9(RX_SEL) and JP10(TX_SEL) to RX1 and TX1  header.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP4 (LED0 EN).
-   Close jumper JP5 (LED1 EN).

If using the MAX32666FTHR (FTHR):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J2 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32670") { %>
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Select RX0 and TX0 on Headers JP3 and JP4 (UART 0).
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP1 (LED1 EN).
-   Close jumper JP2 (LED2 EN).
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32672") { %>
If using the MAX32672EVKIT (EvKit\_V1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Select RX0 and TX0 on Headers JP10 and JP11 (UART 0).
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.

If using the MAX32672FTHR (FTHR):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J4 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32675c") { %>
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP1 (LED0 EN).
-   Close jumper JP2 (LED1 EN).
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max32690") { %>
Connect a MAXPICO or other supported Debug adapter to the SWD Connector.
-   Note: Debug adapters other than the MAXPICO may not route the UART signals to the SWD connector. On MAX32690FTHR and AD-APARD32690-SL boards, this may limit your ability to access the serial port.

If using the MAX32690EVKIT:
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN2 (USB/PWR - UART) connector.
-   Install JP7(RX_EN) and JP8(TX_EN) headers.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP5 (LED0 EN).
-   Close jumper JP6 (LED1 EN).

If using the MAX32690FTHR:
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the J5 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.

If using the AD-APARD32690-SL:
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the P10 (USB-C) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the MAXPICO's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max78000") { %>
If using the MAX78000EVKIT (EvKit\_V1):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Connect pins 1 and 2 (P0_1) of the JH1 (UART 0 EN) header.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP1 (LED1 EN).
-   Close jumper JP2 (LED2 EN).

If using the MAX78000FTHR (FTHR\_RevA):
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN1 (USB/PWR) connector.
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the board's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
<% } %>
<% if (soc === "max78002") { %>
-   Connect a MAXPICO or other supported Debug adapter to the SWD Connector.
-   Connect a USB cable between the PC and the CN2 (USB/UART) connector.
-   Connect the 5V power cable at (5V IN).
-   Close jumper (RX - P0.0) and (TX - P0.1) at Headers JP20 (UART 0 EN).
-   Open a terminal application on the PC and connect to the EV kit's console UART at 115200, 8-N-1.
-   Close jumper JP44 (LED0 EN).
-   Close jumper JP45 (LED1 EN).
<% } %>

## Expected Output

The Console UART of the device will output these messages:

```
Hello World!
count = 0
count = 1
count = 2
count = 3
```


You will also observe <%= ledValue() %> blinking at a rate of <%= blinkRate() %>Hz.
